On 2007-07-23, Rachel Elizabeth Dillon <rachel@akrasiac.org> wrote:
> On 2007-07-23, Derek Ray <derek@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org> wrote:
>> People often make the joke that "Slash'Em wants you dead."  I don't
>> really want that to be Spork at all; I'd just like to make it a Nethack
>> with some more variety and some more stuff in general.  
>
> My understanding of your goals --- and one of my goals, though maybe you
> don't share it --- is also to make it a Nethack that doesn't get really
> easy at the end. 

Yes.  That's true as well, and why some of my changes (such as the
to-hit mods) come in on a curve so their full effect only starts to pop
up in the late game.

The early game can be sufficiently hard for anyone, certainly new
players; it doesn't need to have harsh things added.  But the late game,
as you mention a few lines down, is simply a no-brainer from the Castle
forward.

> While I don't think the right solution to powerful
> items or strategies is just "make them not good anymore," right now
> (for me, anyway, and for others I've talked to) the endgame is really a
> cakewalk, except maybe the Planes. I think that you (Derek) are working
> toward changing that with things like the open Gehennoma nd a couple of
> new monsters; making things like MR, reflection, and elemental resistances
> not 100% effective also seems to work toward this goal.

Yes.  The idea is not to make the game noticeably _harder_ per se
(though some changes will do so unavoidably), but instead to make it
more _challenging_.  Slash'EM, to me, made the game just flat _harder_
without necessarily giving players the resources to deal with it; I'd
prefer that my changes simply require players to find or use existing
alternate solutions, rather than simply bashing through with the
"standard known good".

For example, the Elbereth change.  Elbereth is pretty significant in the
later game, because things simply are going to respect it a lot less;
you can no longer engrave it to give yourself time, breathing room, and
space as an automatic response to "summon nasties".  But nine games
have already been ascended on my server... largely by finding alternate
solutions to late-game troubles other than "burn E, stand on it, and
bash things in a predetermined order of @, A, minotaur, everything
else."

I have faith in Nethack players' ingenuity.  Unless I make something
directly impossible, I am sure that a resourceful player will figure
out ways to mitigate or reduce what might at first glance seem to be a
ridiculously hard situation; look at the post elsewhere, where someone
managed to pry free of an arch-lich, Yeeny, and an E-proof Demogorgon.

Previously, for a skilled player, the solution was "Burn E, kill all
three in melee, move on 20 turns later as if nothing happened."  The 
no-brainer's gone... but that doesn't mean there aren't several other 
solutions available.

